          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is coronary artery disease and how does it develop over time?",
          "answer": "Coronary artery disease is a condition where atherosclerotic plaque builds up in the arteries that supply blood to the heart muscle. This plaque develops over many decades, starting with an injury to the artery lining and the collection of cholesterol-rich material beneath it, according to the document \"Coronary artery disease\".\n\nThe disease can progress in a couple of ways that lead to symptoms:\n*   **Gradual narrowing:** The plaque can slowly narrow the artery. This limits blood flow when the heart needs more, like during exercise, causing predictable chest discomfort known as stable angina.\n*   **Plaque rupture:** A plaque can rupture, exposing material that triggers the formation of a blood clot. This clot can suddenly block the artery, leading to an acute coronary syndrome, such as a heart attack. This can happen even with plaques that weren't causing symptoms before.\n\nThe ICD-10 classification system reflects this progression, with codes for ischemic heart disease (coronary artery disease) ranging from angina to acute myocardial infarction (heart attack) and then to the chronic atherosclerotic disease that follows, as described in \"ICD-10-CM Chapter 9: diseases of the circulatory system (I00-I99)\".\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
